  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 9,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.049,
            0.432,
            0.066
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 7 | Part-II"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.176,
            0.088,
            0.658,
            0.108
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Kabamai will arrive at the sweet shop again after"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.345,
            0.119,
            0.652,
            0.139
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "10, 20, 30, 40, 50, 60, 70, ... days."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.147,
            0.405,
            0.166
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "These are multiples of 10."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.166,
            0.847,
            0.223
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "When will Kabamai eat free sweets again? It will happen on days common to the sequences of multiples above. It can be seen that this will first happen after 70 days."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.224,
            0.847,
            0.262
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Notice that, here too, 70 is the lowest among all the common multiples of 7 and 10."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.174,
            0.273,
            0.846,
            0.293
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "For both these problems the solution was the lowest common multiple."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.294,
            0.847,
            0.331
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Lowest Common Multiple (LCM)",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " of two or more given numbers is the lowest (or smallest or least) of their common multiples."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.341,
            0.847,
            0.532
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "? Do you remember the ‘Idli-Vada’ game from Grade 6 (see chapter ‘Prime Time’)? Two numbers are chosen and whenever players come to their multiples, ‘idli’ or ‘vada’ should be called out depending on whose multiple the number is. If the number happens to be a common multiple, then ‘idli-vada’ should be called out. In each problem below, the two numbers corresponding to ‘idli’ and ‘vada’ are given. Find the first number for which ‘idli-vada’ will be called out: (a) 4 and 6 (b) 7 and 11 (c) 14 and 30 (d) 15 and 55"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.542,
            0.732,
            0.563
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Is the answer always the LCM of the two numbers? Explain."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.577,
            0.846,
            0.636
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "As in the case of the HCF, the process of finding the LCM by listing down the multiples may get tedious for larger numbers, as you would have seen for questions (c) and (d) above."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.64,
            0.843,
            0.66
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Prime factorisation can simplify the process of finding the LCM as well."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.669,
            0.81,
            0.692
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "？ How do we find the LCM of two numbers using their prime factors?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.713,
            0.657,
            0.735
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Finding LCM through Prime Factorisation"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.74,
            0.847,
            0.816
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "We have seen that every factor of a number is formed by taking a subpart of its prime factorisation. We used this fact to come up with a method to find the HCF of two numbers. In a similar manner, we can come up with a method to find the LCM."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.817,
            0.847,
            0.874
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "We begin by comparing the prime factorisations of a number and a multiple of that number. For example, let us take 36 and its multiple 648 (=36 × 18)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.161,
            0.934,
            0.187,
            0.949
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "56"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
